earch_pages_by_keyword caused an error, I should avoid using it again. Instead, I can manually navigate through the site to find the post. The tree shows a searchbox in the complementary section. I can use the search functionality to find the post. I'll interact with the searchbox: locate it by role 'searchbox' with name 'Search', fill it with the post title, and click the search button. Then, I'll wait for results and click on the link to the post. After navigating to the post, I'll use submit_comment_on_page to leave the comment. This approach avoids the problematic search function and directly uses the UI. I must ensure to handle the search interaction correctly and then proceed to comment.\n</reasoning>\n\n<code>\nasync def act(page):\n    # Step 1: Use the search box to find the target post\n    searchbox = page.get_by_role(\"searchbox\", name=\"Search\")\n    await searchbox.fill(\"Settle Down, Nerds. AI Is A Normal Technology\")\n    search_button = page.get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Search\")\n    await search_button.click()\n    await page.wait_for_load_state(\"domcontentloaded\")\n    \n    # Step 2: After search, find and click the link to the post\n    # Assuming the search results show the post as a link with the title\n    post_link = page.get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Settle Down, Nerds. AI Is A Normal Technology\", exact=False)\n    await post_link.click()\n    await page.wait_for_load_state(\"domcontentloaded\")\n    \n    # Step 3: Submit the comment using the knowledge base function\n    current_url = page.url\n    # Extract relative URL from current_url (assuming it's like '/?p=...')\n    import urllib.parse\n    parsed_url = urllib.parse.urlparse(current_url)\n    post_url = parsed_url.path + (\"?\" + parsed_url.query if parsed_url.query else \"\")\n    if not post_url:\n        post_url = \"/\"  # fallback\n    \n    comment_text = \"Appreciate the detailed explanation.\"\n    commenter_name = \"Test User\"\n    commenter_email = \"test@example